<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#war-elephant">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">War elephant</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as war elephants</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A war elephant is an elephant trained for combat, used to charge enemy formations, carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and intimidate opponents. They were especially important in Indian warfare, notably in the Maurya and Gupta empires, and spread to the Hellenistic world through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a> famously used them in its wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and they also appeared in the armies of the Sasanian, Aksumite, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> and Mughal empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-elephant.jpg" alt="War elephant" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri-Paul Motte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_traverse_le_Rh%C3%B4ne_Henri_Motte_1878.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
